<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Farrukh Yasar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1500">1500</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Farrukh Yasar was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> from the 1460s until 1500, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> as an ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>. In 1488, with Aq Qoyunlu help, his forces defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Shaykh Haydar</a>, leader of the militant Safavid order, who had invaded Shirvan. In 1500 Haydar's son Ismail, the future Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, defeated and killed him in revenge.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/farrukh-yasar.jpg" alt="Farrukh Yasar" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gulustan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A4%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%80%D1%83%D1%85_%D0%AF%D1%81%D0%B0%D1%80.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Farrukh Yasar was the last independent ruler of Shirvan. Breaking with his ancestors' alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>, he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and married his daughter Gawhar-Sultan Khanum to Uzun Hasan's son Yaqub; the later Aq Qoyunlu sultans Baysunghur and Murad were his grandsons. On 31 January 1468 the forces of Shirvan and the Aq Qoyunlu together defeated the Timurid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-mirza">Abu Sa'id Mirza</a>. He also sought ties with Mehmed the Conqueror, sending an envoy and his merchant Khawja Yar Ahmad to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> with gold.</p><p>In 1465 his envoy Hasan Shirvani went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> answered with an embassy under Vasily Papin, accompanied by merchants including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afanasy-nikitin">Afanasy Nikitin</a>, to seek trade and a pact against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. In 1488 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Shaykh Haydar</a>, marching through Shirvan towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> on the pretext of a war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a>, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>, took the capital and burned it. Unable to defend it, Farrukh Yasar called on his son-in-law Sultan Yaqub. On 9 July 1488 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> under Suleiman Bey and the Shirvan forces led by the shah himself defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>; Haydar was wounded by an arrow, captured and beheaded, and his head was sent to Yaqub.</p><p>After Yaqub died in 1490, Farrukh Yasar backed his grandson Baysunghur, who fled to Shirvan when he was deposed in 1492 and was later killed in battle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barda">Barda</a> and Ganja; Baysunghur's brother Murad also took refuge in Shirvan until 1497. That year the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Shaki</a>, Shah Husain, rebelled with the shah's youngest son, the future Ibrahim II Shaykhshah, and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qabala">Gabala</a> for eight days; Farrukh Yasar crushed the revolt but pardoned them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Haydar's son Ismail, freed in 1492 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a>, set out to avenge his father and in 1499 began his march on Shirvan. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jabani">Battle of Jabani</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> the Shirvan army was destroyed and Farrukh Yasar was captured and beheaded; Ismail took Shamakhi and moved on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. Many sources remember him as a merciful ruler, and he patronised the building of the Tuba Shahi Mosque and the Juma Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>. His descendants ruled Shirvan under Safavid suzerainty until 1538, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a> appointed its first Safavid governor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Farrukh Yasar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1460 Shaykh Junayd, the order's militant leader, was killed in battle with the forces of Khalilullah I, and in 1488 his son Shaykh Haydar was killed by <b>Farrukh Yasar</b>, who was aided by the Aq Qoyunlu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Baku, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.37,49.84&amp;year=1490" aria-label="Baku on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5774 721 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Farrukh Yasar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6224 991h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6224 991h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.37,49.84&amp;year=1490">What was here in 1490? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
